>> "something_params" syntax and when one uses the "=position_jitter(" type
>> syntax?
>>
>>

>> Apparently, this syntax is not readily documented (the help page for layer sends you to the website which is silent on it). οΏ½It is documented in the book, page 42. οΏ½There it lists the arguments for layer as: geom, geom_params, stat, stat_params, data, mapping, position. οΏ½So only geom and stat take their params as a list as a separate argument. position is either a string or a position_* function. Dive into the source code and you will find that there are also arguments params, ..., inherit.aes, legend, and subset

>>
>>
>> For instance, changing the "geom" part of the syntax so that the layer
>> reads
>>
>> layer2<-layer(
>> data=DAAT
>> , mapping=aes(x=X, y=zero)
>> , geom=geom_point(shape=1)
>> , position=position_jitter(height=0, width=0)
>> )
>> print(
>> ggplot() + layer2
>> + scale_y_continuous(name="", expand=c(0,1), breaks=NA)
>> )
>>
>> would seem to be more consistent, but it returns an error.
>>
>>

>> Right. οΏ½Because geom and stat are to be strings with their parameters in geom_params and stat_params while position can be either a string or the function with params.

>>
>>
>> Setting "breaks=NA" removes both the tick *and* the horizontal line at
>> y=zero. Is there a way to remove only the tick? This would be like saying
>>
>> axis(2, tick=FALSE)
>>
>> in traditional graphics.
>>
>>
>> Here is a workaround:
>>
>> layer2<-layer(data=DAAT,

>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½mapping=aes(x=X, y=zero),
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½geom="point",
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½geom_params=list(shape=1),
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½position=position_jitter(height=2))
>> print(
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ggplot() + layer2 +
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½scale_y_continuous(name="", expand=c(0,1), breaks=0) +
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½opts(axis.ticks = theme_blank(),
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ axis.text.y = theme_blank())
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½)
>>
>>
>> I set the only break a 0, which puts a major grid line there. Formatting the ticks and labels is part of the theme system, and so is controlled with the opts. οΏ½A downside is that there is a single option for ticks (axis.ticks) which controls both the x and y axis. οΏ½You can't theme the y ticks separate from the x ones. οΏ½Setting the y text to blank does get rid of the 0 label.

>>
>>
>> One workaround is to use the "hline" geom, although the line is not the
>> same as the white "grid" line that ggplot2 naturally draws:
>>
>> print(
>> ggplot() + layer2
>> + scale_y_continuous(name="", expand=c(0,1), breaks=NA)
>> + geom_hline(mapping=aes(yintercept=0))
>> )
>>
>> How would one place the above into full layer syntax? Defining
>>
>> layer3<-layer(
>> geom="hline"
>> , stat="identity"
>> , geom_params=list( mapping=aes(yintercept=0))
>> )
>> print(ggplot() + layer2 + layer3)
>>
>> appears to be incorrect as it draws no horizontal line at all.
>>
>>
>> layer3 <- layer(data=data.frame(yintercept=0),

>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½mapping=aes(yintercept=yintercept),
>>  οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½ οΏ½geom="hline")
>>
>> I figured this out by exploring the layer object returned by geom_hline(yintercept=1) and by looking at the source code. οΏ½Apparently, yintercept must be turned into an aesthetic, which geom_hline does under the hood. οΏ½Also note the default stat for hline is "hline", not "identity".
>>
>>
>> Editorial: I applaud your wanting to understand ggplot2 at the layer level. οΏ½It's something I've wanted to do more as well. οΏ½I've had the impression that the layer level is the most pure expression of the grammar, with the geom_* and stat_* built on that for convenience, and the qplot level obscuring everything but giving people a chance to transition. οΏ½However, starting to look at it, I'm beginning to wonder if I should think of layer (the function) as an implementation detail, inasmuch as it makes assumptions that the higher level functions guarantee and it really isn't meant to be called directly. οΏ½If you continue down this path, you will have to use the source and understand how the system is put together at the programming level; also, there is no guarantee that things won't change. οΏ½In fact, I know that the re-write which is happening changes much of how these things are put together at the lower levels, but is supposed to maintain the "API" of the geom_* and stat_* levels.

