Well I think I found a solution, although it feels a bit clunky, so I'd still be interested in hearing other solutions.  I used the fillable dot shape (shape=21) so that I could use the scale_fill_identity to specify the dot colors and the legend and used scale_colour_identity to specify the box colors.  This has the mildly annoying feature of leaving the dots with a black border in the legend, but I can certainly live with that.  Here's the code I ended up with in case anyone is interested.

attrib.by.brand<-structure(list(Attribute = structure(c(1L, 1L, 2L, 2L), .Label = c("a1",
                "a2"), class = "factor"), Brand = structure(c(1L, 2L, 1L, 2L),
                .Label = c("b1","b2"), class = "factor"), Rating = c(50L, 60L, 70L, 80L),
                colors = structure(c(2L,1L, 2L, 1L), .Label = c("blue", "red"),
                class = "factor"), sigColor = structure(c(1L,2L, 1L, 2L), .Label = c("black", "grey"),
                class = "factor")), .Names = c("Attribute","Brand", "Rating", "colors", "sigColor"),
                class = "data.frame", row.names = c(NA,-4L))

colorsScale<-unique(attrib.by.brand$colors)
brandNames<-unique(attrib.by.brand$Brand)
brandNames2<-factor(brandNames, levels=brandNames[order(colorsScale)],ordered=T)

plot<-ggplot(attrib.by.brand,aes(Rating,Attribute))+
  geom_point(size=4,shape=21,aes(fill=colors,color=colors))+
  geom_point(size=8,shape=22,aes(colour=sigColor))+
  scale_colour_identity()+
  scale_fill_identity(guide="legend",labels=levels(brandNames2))

plot

